WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
In Swift, you have a well-defined built-in stack.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.840
URL Session handles the network.

3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.120
Combine manages reactive state.

4
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.160
Core data handles persistence.

5
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.400
Apple gives you the tools and they fit together by design.

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.920
React Native doesn't ship with an equivalent monolithic stack.

7
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.080
Instead, the native blocks splinters into a fragmented ecosystem of third-party packages.

8
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.400
Out of the box, you won't find a direct built-in replacement for URL Session,

9
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.080
Combine, or Core data.

10
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.360
But that fragmentation is deceiving.

11
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.120
Over time, the React Native community has converged on a very specific set of libraries

12
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.760
that map almost one-to-one with the iOS tools you already know.

13
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.560
We are going to map the standard React Native stack directly against the Swift architecture.

14
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.760
We will fill those gaps with Axios, TanStack Query, ZooStand, and MMKV.

15
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.000
Architectural rigor in React Native comes from the intentional composition of these third-party equivalents,

16
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.480
recreating the predictable data flow found in a native stack.

17
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.720
Let's start at the network layer.

18
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.560
Axios is the standard fetch library in React Native,

19
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.640
filling the exact role of URL Session and AlamoFire.

20
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.120
Just like AlamoFire, Axios supports a structured request pipeline through interceptors,

21
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.720
mapping perfectly to AlamoFire's request interceptor.

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.440
The Adapt step, your request interceptor,

23
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.480
is exactly where you attach authorization headers before the request hits the network.

24
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.800
This diagram shows how the response interceptor handles the retry step.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.200
Notice the token refresh queue on the Axios side.

26
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.360
This gate holds back outgoing requests while a new token is fetched,

27
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.120
preventing multiple simultaneous refresh calls,

28
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.000
the same race condition you guard against in Swift.

29
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.680
Moving up from raw networking, we hit server-state management.

30
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.000
In SwiftUI, you'd reach for a combined publisher on an observable object.

31
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.760
In React Native, you use TanStack Query.

32
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.560
Many beginners try to read server data by calling a raw JavaScript fetch

33
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.760
directly inside a UI component using the use effect hook.

34
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:08.080
Calling Async Pode like this is the exact equivalent of firing an unmanaged task in Swift.

35
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.240
You have no structured cancellation, no retry logic, and no background refetch management.

36
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.880
TanStack Query takes over all of that lifecycle management.

37
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.840
You hand it your Async function and the library automatically handles loading states, error catching,

38
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.200
caching, and stale while revalidate protocols.

39
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.880
To keep the cache organized, you use a query key factory,

40
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.320
a centralized registry for all your network requests.

41
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.880
By centralizing query keys and hooking them to use query,

42
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.800
you map exactly to the iOS pattern of grouping combined publishers by domain.

43
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.960
It prevents typos and makes cache invalidation highly predictable.

44
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.440
TanStack Query abstracts server state completely away from the view layer.

45
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.480
It restores the clean, decoupling iOS engineers expect from combined,

46
00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.920
without writing the boilerplate yourself.

47
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.280
We now hit the most obvious architectural divergence.

48
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.440
React Native has no built-in offline first persistence layer.

49
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.680
There is no native core data or Swift data equivalent out of the box.

50
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.640
To solve this, the community splits the problem strictly into two,

51
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.120
server state for backend data and local client state for UI data.

52
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.560
TanStack Query owns the server state.

53
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.480
It's cache effectively serves as the in-memory equivalent of a core data NS-margined object context.

54
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.160
For local client state, you use ZewStand.

55
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.840
Think of it as a lightweight observable object that holds state and exposes actions,

56
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.320
completely skipping the combined boilerplate.

57
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.800
You will often see React's built-in context API used for local state,

58
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.920
but you have to know exactly when to choose context over ZewStand.

59
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.000
Context is a blunt instrument.

60
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.280
Whenever any part of its value changes,

61
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.160
it forces a full re-render of every single consumer component listening to it.

62
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.320
When global context updates, the entire child tree re-renders.

63
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.800
ZewStand selector pattern subscribes two specific slices,

64
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.840
re-rendering only when that exact value changes.

65
00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.680
This precision handles rapid runtime changes like active carts.

66
00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.040
Reserve context for static, low-frequency data like theme objects or routing instances.

67
00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.000
Down at the lightweight persistence layer,

68
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.200
user defaults maps directly to the React Native MMKV package.

69
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.960
MMKV gives you the exact same synchronous read and write API as user defaults,

70
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.720
but it uses memory-mapped files instead of SQLite.

71
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.600
It is exceptionally fast.

72
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.640
For secrets and encrypted bundle storage,

73
00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.560
the iOS keychain maps to expo secure store.

74
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.720
There is one operational cache.

75
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.360
Secure store is synchronous on the native side,

76
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.760
but to cross the JavaScript bridge, it returns promises.

77
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.760
You must always await it.

78
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.440
By composing tan stacks cache, ZewStand slices and MMKV,

79
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.320
you achieve a highly modular state architecture

80
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.280
that handles complex data flows without the heavy overhead of a traditional ORM.

81
00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.680
Now let's look at application stability.

82
00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.400
Swift makes errors explicit at the type level with result success failure.

83
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.200
JavaScript has no equivalent.

84
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.600
Errors simply propagate as implicit-brone exceptions.

85
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.280
TanStack Query cleanly normalizes these implicit exceptions.

86
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.320
Network failures surface through a simple is error property on your query,

87
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.520
catching and routing them critically.

88
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.680
For unexpected UI exceptions that occur during a render,

89
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.800
React provides error boundaries.

90
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.400
These act as a fallback safety net to catch unhandled crashes in your component tree.

91
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.600
You should place these error boundaries strictly at the edges of your navigation screens.

92
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.800
This isolates the failure, ensuring a crash in one minor component

93
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.000
doesn't tear down the entire application shell.

94
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.800
Once stability is handled, you can implement advanced interactions like optimistic updates.

95
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.720
TanStack Query automates this through a three-callback pattern,

96
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.960
on mutate, on error, and on settled.

97
00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.520
Here is the flow.

98
00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.280
On mutate instantly updates the local state,

99
00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:01.120
so the UI feels perfectly responsive.

100
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.840
If the network fails, on error automatically triggers a state rollback.

101
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.840
Finally, on settled, reconciles the local cache with the true server state.

102
00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.160
This built-in pattern automates the cache consistency logic

103
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.840
that normally requires manual coordination

104
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.800
when building optimistic updates from scratch in iOS.

105
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.560
Let's pull back and look at the fully constructed feature module.

106
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.800
The architecture stacks neatly.

107
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.080
Your API.ts file houses the Axios network instance.

108
00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.920
TanStack Query manages the server cache.

109
00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.840
ZooStand holds the transient UI state

110
00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:41.200
and MMKV and secure store lock down local preferences.

111
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.480
This separation of logic mirrors the exact boundaries you establish

112
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:51.080
when composing URL session, combine, core data, and user defaults.

113
00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.400
Each tool has a strictly bounded responsibility.

114
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.840
Data moves predictably through the pipeline,

115
00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.560
completely avoiding a tangled monolithic state container.

116
00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.680
Framework shock is usually just syntax deep.

117
00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.280
Once you understand how the React Native ecosystem converges,

118
00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.640
you can take the exact same structural rigor

119
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.360
you rely on in iOS and apply it directly

120
00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.760
to your JavaScript architecture.

